# Rust sources are checked out with CRLF endings on every platform, and this
# is load-bearing rather than a style preference.
#
# Roughly fifteen tests in this crate are source pins: they read a file off
# disk and match on its bytes -- module boundaries, marker comments, struct
# declarations, a byte-length-plus-hash pin over Cargo.toml. Several of those
# markers carry a line terminator inside the pattern. Some readers normalize
# CRLF to LF first (job_object.rs, below_cut.rs, login_ui.rs, and login_ui
# goes further and asserts both forms); most do not.
#
# Measured, not assumed: a clone taken with core.autocrlf=false gives an LF
# working tree, and 23 tests fail on it -- reported as "contains 0 occurrences
# of the test-module marker, not 1", which reads like a corrupted file rather
# than a line-ending mismatch. That is the state a contributor on Linux or
# macOS gets by default, and the state a Windows contributor gets from a
# single git config they had a good reason to set.
#
# All 84 .rs files are stored LF in the repository, so this changes nothing
# about what is committed. It fixes what is checked out.
*.rs text eol=crlf
